exports[`HTML Render Snapshot Tests > Chapter 1 HTML output matches snapshot 1`] = `
[
  {
    "html": "<p>Welcome to your first active speaking lesson! In this chapter, we will learn how
to greet others, introduce ourselves, and discuss family relations.</p>
<p>Rather than speaking in a stiff, robotic manner, you will learn to use essential
Cantonese conversational particles from day one to sound natural, polite, and
culturally authentic.</p>
<hr>
<h2>1. Natural Greetings</h2>
<p>To say hello in Cantonese, the standard phrase is <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="670d9743542cae3e">你好<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5hou2</strong><br/>hello</span></span>.
However, in casual, everyday speech, saying just <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="670d9743542cae3e">你好<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5hou2</strong><br/>hello</span></span> can
sound a bit formal or detached. To make it sound warm and friendly, native
speakers almost always append the softening particle
<span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="03fe6b38e7fc21ba">呀<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3</strong><br/>sentence-final particle softening statements, making questions friendly</span></span>
to the end:</p>
",
    "type": "prose",
  },
  {
    "html": "<span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="670d9743542cae3e">你好<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5hou2</strong><br/>hello</span></span><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="03fe6b38e7fc21ba">呀<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3</strong><br/>sentence-final particle softening statements, making questions friendly</span></span>！
===
Hello there!",
    "type": "cantonese",
  },
  {
    "html": "<h3>Other Everyday Greetings</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="119af509ad0770c5">早晨<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>zou2san4</strong><br/>good morning</span></span>: The standard way to say &quot;good morning.&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="5c90d32e43b417f8">早唞<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>zou2tau2</strong><br/>good night</span></span>: Used when going to bed.</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="40b65f7c83f4a2af">拜拜<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>baai1baai3</strong><br/>bye-bye</span></span>: The most common colloquial way to say goodbye.</li>
</ul>
<p>Observe a simple polite exchange:</p>
",
    "type": "prose",
  },
  {
    "html": "A: <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="670d9743542cae3e">你好<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5hou2</strong><br/>hello</span></span><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="03fe6b38e7fc21ba">呀<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3</strong><br/>sentence-final particle softening statements, making questions friendly</span></span>！ <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="119af509ad0770c5">早晨<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>zou2san4</strong><br/>good morning</span></span>！
   === Hello there! Good morning!
B: <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="119af509ad0770c5">早晨<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>zou2san4</strong><br/>good morning</span></span>！ <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="a0c7716669b5ded0">你<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5</strong><br/>you</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="f867f34178594f89">好<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hou2</strong><br/>good / very</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="fa474ee4a665e443">嗎<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>maa3</strong><br/>question particle</span></span>？
   === Good morning! How are you?",
    "type": "dialog",
  },
  {
    "html": "<hr>
<h2>2. Personal Pronouns &amp; The Equative Verb <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span></h2>
<h3>Pronouns</h3>
<p>Cantonese pronouns are extremely logical and consistent. To make any singular
pronoun plural, you simply attach the suffix <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="fe9438051c80b6ab">哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>dei6</strong><br/>plural suffix</span></span>, which
acts as a group marker:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Singular Pronouns</strong>:<ul>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="b70bb4acc0484cf0">我<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ngo5</strong><br/>I</span></span>: &quot;I&quot; or &quot;me&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="a0c7716669b5ded0">你<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5</strong><br/>you</span></span>: &quot;you&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="7379f21b80edca33">佢<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>keoi5</strong><br/>he / she / it</span></span>: &quot;he&quot;, &quot;she&quot;, or &quot;it&quot;</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Plural Pronouns</strong>:<ul>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="771583ea755aab41">我哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ngo5dei6</strong><br/>we</span></span>: &quot;we&quot; or &quot;us&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="a23d45e1195dc3e3">你哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>nei5dei6</strong><br/>you (plural)</span></span>: &quot;you (plural)&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="d7b451126e595e8a">佢哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>keoi5dei6</strong><br/>they</span></span>: &quot;they&quot; or &quot;them&quot;</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>General &amp; Reference Pronouns</strong>:<ul>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="16b54b7ab3b5e984">大家<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>daai6gaa1</strong><br/>everyone</span></span>: &quot;everyone&quot;</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="fb43354d2aaece51">自己<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>zi6gei2</strong><br/>oneself</span></span>: &quot;oneself / own&quot;</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<div class="alert-box alert-note">
      <div class="alert-title">NOTE</div>
      <div class="alert-content"><p>Cantonese does not differentiate gender in its spoken pronouns. The
word <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="7379f21b80edca33">佢<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>keoi5</strong><br/>he / she / it</span></span> is used for &quot;he&quot;, &quot;she&quot;, and &quot;it&quot; alike.</p></div>
    </div>
<h3>The Equative <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span></h3>
<p>To express &quot;to be&quot; (am, is, are), use the verb <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span>. The
standard sentence structure is: <strong>Subject + <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span> +
Identity/Noun</strong></p>
<p>For example:</p>
",
    "type": "prose",
  },
  {
    "html": "<span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="b70bb4acc0484cf0">我<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ngo5</strong><br/>I</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="d448d143c4e04eb6">香港<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hoeng1gong2</strong><br/>Hong Kong</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="50f5d65d57290f75">人<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>jan4</strong><br/>person</span></span>。
===
I am a Hong Konger.",
    "type": "cantonese",
  },
  {
    "html": "<h3>The Possessive Particle <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="6ddec4a1f3da9adc">嘅<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ge3</strong><br/>possessive / linking particle</span></span></h3>
<p>To show possession or relationship (like &quot;&#39;s&quot; or &quot;of&quot; in English), attach the
possessive particle <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="6ddec4a1f3da9adc">嘅<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ge3</strong><br/>possessive / linking particle</span></span> after a pronoun or
noun:</p>
",
    "type": "prose",
  },
  {
    "html": "<span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="d7b451126e595e8a">佢哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>keoi5dei6</strong><br/>they</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="771583ea755aab41">我哋<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ngo5dei6</strong><br/>we</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="6ddec4a1f3da9adc">嘅<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ge3</strong><br/>possessive / linking particle</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="677aad1a0ee44cd6">朋友<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>pang4jau5</strong><br/>friend</span></span>。
===
They are our friends.",
    "type": "cantonese",
  },
  {
    "html": "<h3>Family &amp; Kinship Vocabulary</h3>
<p>Here are some common family members you can use to describe relationships:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="37042cdb90ebde28">父母<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>fu6mou5</strong><br/>parents</span></span>: parents</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="85bf74961e252a34">阿哥<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3go1</strong><br/>older brother</span></span>: older brother</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="eb6476721a76ac38">家姐<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>gaa1ze1</strong><br/>older sister</span></span>: older sister</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="b3fec4eebec79120">細佬<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>sai3lou2</strong><br/>younger brother</span></span>: younger brother</li>
<li><span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="b9cade467ab934f8">阿妹<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3mui2</strong><br/>younger sister</span></span>: younger sister</li>
</ul>
<p>Using the possessive particle <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="6ddec4a1f3da9adc">嘅<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ge3</strong><br/>possessive / linking particle</span></span>, you can
introduce your family members:</p>
",
    "type": "prose",
  },
  {
    "html": "<span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="730bcaa66436879d">呢個<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ni1go3</strong><br/>this one</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="cfcbbb219389def4">係<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>hai6</strong><br/>to be / yes</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="b70bb4acc0484cf0">我<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ngo5</strong><br/>I</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="6ddec4a1f3da9adc">嘅<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>ge3</strong><br/>possessive / linking particle</span></span> <span class="vocab-term" data-audio-hash="85bf74961e252a34">阿哥<span class="tooltip-popover"><strong>aa3go1</strong><br/>older brother</span></span>。
===
This is my older brother.",
    "type": "cantonese",
  },
  {
